DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The lliibbccaassppeerr library provides for the control of application capabili‐
     ties through the casper process.

     An application capability, represented by the _c_a_p___c_h_a_n_n_e_l___t type, is a
     communication channel between the caller and the casper daemon or an in‐
     stance of one of the daemon's services.  A capability to the casper
     process, obtained with the ccaapp__iinniitt() function, allows a program to cre‐
     ate capabilities to access the casper daemon's services via the
     ccaapp__sseerrvviiccee__ooppeenn() function.

     The ccaapp__iinniitt() function instantiates a capability to allow a program to
     access the casper daemon.

     The ccaapp__wwrraapp() function creates a _c_a_p___c_h_a_n_n_e_l___t based on the socket sup‐
     plied in the call.  The function is used when a capability is inherited
     through the execve(2) system call, or sent over a unix(4) domain socket
     as a file descriptor, and has to be converted into a _c_a_p___c_h_a_n_n_e_l___t.  The
     _f_l_a_g_s argument defines the channel behavior.  The supported flags are:

           CASPER_NO_UNIQ
           The communication between the process and the casper daemon uses no
           unique version of nvlist.

     The ccaapp__uunnwwrraapp() function returns the unix(4) domain socket used by the
     daemon service, and frees the _c_a_p___c_h_a_n_n_e_l___t structure.

     The ccaapp__cclloonnee() function returns a clone of the capability passed as its
     only argument.

     The ccaapp__cclloossee() function closes, and frees, the given capability.

     The ccaapp__ssoocckk() function returns the unix(4) domain socket descriptor as‐
     sociated with the given capability for use with system calls such as:
     kevent(2), poll(2), and select(2).

     The ccaapp__lliimmiitt__ggeett() function stores the current limits of the given capa‐
     bility in the _l_i_m_i_t_s_p argument.  If the function returns _0 and NULL is
     stored in the _l_i_m_i_t_s_p argument, there are no limits set.

     The ccaapp__lliimmiitt__sseett() function sets limits for the given capability.  The
     limits are provided as an nvlist(9).  The exact format of the limits de‐
     pends on the service that the capability represents.  ccaapp__lliimmiitt__sseett()
     frees the limits passed to the call, whether or not the operation suc‐
     ceeds or fails.

     The ccaapp__sseenndd__nnvvlliisstt() function sends the given nvlist(9) over the given
     capability.  This is a low level interface to communicate with casper
     services.  It is expected that most services will provide a higher level
     API.

     The ccaapp__rreeccvv__nnvvlliisstt() function receives the given nvlist(9) over the
     given capability.

     The ccaapp__xxffeerr__nnvvlliisstt() function sends the given nvlist(9), destroys it,
     and receives a new nvlist(9) in response over the given capability.  It
     does not matter if the function succeeds or fails, the nvlist(9) given
     for sending will always be destroyed before the function returns.

     The ccaapp__sseerrvviiccee__ooppeenn() function opens the casper service named in the
     call using the casper capability obtained via the ccaapp__iinniitt() function.
     The ccaapp__sseerrvviiccee__ooppeenn() function returns a capability that provides access
     to the opened service.  Casper supports the following services in the
     base system:

           system.dns     provides libc compatible DNS API
           system.fileargs
                          provides an API for opening files specified on a
                          command line
           system.grp     provides a getgrent(3) compatible API
           system.net     provides a libc compatible network API
           system.netdb   provides libc compatible network proto API
           system.pwd     provides a getpwent(3) compatible API
           system.sysctl  provides a sysctlbyname(3) compatible API
           system.syslog  provides a syslog(3) compatible API

     ccaapp__iinniitt() must be called from a single-threaded context.  ccaapp__cclloonnee(),
     ccaapp__cclloossee(), ccaapp__lliimmiitt__ggeett(), ccaapp__lliimmiitt__sseett(), ccaapp__sseenndd__nnvvlliisstt(),
     ccaapp__rreeccvv__nnvvlliisstt(), and ccaapp__sseerrvviiccee__ooppeenn() are reentrant but not thread-
     safe.  That is, they may be called from separate threads only with dif‐
     ferent _c_a_p___c_h_a_n_n_e_l___t arguments or with synchronization.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ccaapp__cclloonnee(), ccaapp__iinniitt(), ccaapp__rreeccvv__nnvvlliisstt(), ccaapp__sseerrvviiccee__ooppeenn(),
     ccaapp__wwrraapp() and ccaapp__xxffeerr__nnvvlliisstt() functions return NULL and set the _e_r_r_n_o
     variable on failure.

     The ccaapp__lliimmiitt__ggeett(), ccaapp__lliimmiitt__sseett() and ccaapp__sseenndd__nnvvlliisstt() functions re‐
     turn -1 and set the _e_r_r_n_o variable on failure.

     The ccaapp__cclloossee(), ccaapp__ssoocckk() and ccaapp__uunnwwrraapp() functions always succeed.

